---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/aviation/foundational/aircraft-systems-and-instruments">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Aviation's first commandment is know your machine. A light aircraft is a simple system —
    engine, controls, a handful of instruments — flown by a single human whose first job is
    understanding what each part does and how it fails. Everything in the FAA's knowledge syllabus
    hangs from that competence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the aircraft as a system, part by part" widget="equationStep" config={systemsCfg} />

  <h2>The six-pack, and what each instrument trusts</h2>
  <p>
    The classic panel is three pitot-static instruments (airspeed, altimeter, vertical speed) and
    three gyroscopic ones (attitude, heading, turn coordinator). The division matters operationally:
    block the pitot tube (ice, a bug) and the airspeed indicator lies quietly; lose vacuum and the
    gyros wind down. Pilots cross-check constantly because instruments fail politely but wrongly.
  </p>

  <h2>Control: three axes, three surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    Roll, pitch and yaw are commanded by ailerons, elevator and rudder respectively — with
    couplings everywhere (banking yaws you; that is why rudder accompanies aileron). Trim tabs
    neutralise control pressure so the aircraft flies hands-off, and the whole system is rigged so
    that stability, not the pilot, is the first line of level flight.
  </p>

  <h2>Preflight: the walkaround</h2>
  <p>
    The preflight inspection is where systems knowledge becomes safety: draining fuel sumps to
    check for water (the number-one avgas contaminant), exercising controls for full and free
    movement, checking surfaces, tyres, oil. It takes ten minutes and prevents the class of
    accidents that never should have started. Aviation's culture — checklists, cross-checks,
    written standards — is the discipline's real technology.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAA Private Pilot ACS-6C and <em>Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge</em> (FAA-H-8083-25).</p>
</TopicPage>
